{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.OrderingIndependence","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.OrderingIndependence","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.GroundRing","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.TransferMatrix","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.BetaDecay","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.YoungIdempotent","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.Q_sym","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.Q_sym","kind":"def","line":154,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrderingIndependence.lean#L154-L167","doc":"The unique, ordering-independent Q-value for β⁻ decay.\n\n`Q_sym = |log_q(P_sym(dau) / P_sym(par))| · ℏ_q · m_e/3`\n\nThis is the ONLY algebraically determined Q-value:\n- It does not depend on the nucleon ordering\n- It is computed over `ℤ[q, q⁻¹]` (no numerical optimization)\n- The structural error (+4.4% for tritium) is the Level 0\n  approximation, not an ordering artifact. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.ordering_independence","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.ordering_independence","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrderingIndependence.lean#L123-L145","doc":"**Theorem (ordering independence).**\nFor any two orderings `σ₁, σ₂` of the same nuclear configuration,\nthe Young-symmetrized Q-value is identical:\n\n`Q_sym(σ₁) = Q_sym(σ₂)`\n\n**Proof sketch:**\n1. `e_{(n)} = (1/P_n(q²)) Σ_{w ∈ S_n} q^{ℓ(w)} T_w`\n2. `e · B(σ₁) · e = e · B(σ₂) · e` because the symmetrizer\n   sums over all permutations, absorbing any specific ordering.\n3. More precisely: `B(σ₂) = T_{w₁₂} · B(σ₁) · T_{w₁₂}⁻¹`\n   where `w₁₂` is the permutation mapping `σ₁ → σ₂`.\n4. `e · T_w · B · T_w⁻¹ · e = e · B · e` because\n   `e · T_w = q^{ℓ(w)} · e` (symmetric eigenvalue). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.skein_multiset_invariance_axiom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.skein_multiset_invariance_axiom","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrderingIndependence.lean#L112-L119","doc":"Per-instance form: the deprecated free-function alias. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.skeinMultisetInvariant_refl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.skeinMultisetInvariant_refl","kind":"instance","line":104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrderingIndependence.lean#L104-L110","doc":"Reflexivity instance: when both configs are the same, the\npolynomials are trivially equal. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SkeinMultisetInvariant.invariance","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SkeinMultisetInvariant.invariance","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrderingIndependence.lean#L102-L102","doc":"Polynomials agree on configurations with the same nucleon multiset. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SkeinMultisetInvariant.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SkeinMultisetInvariant.mk","kind":"ctor","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrderingIndependence.lean#L97-L97","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.SkeinMultisetInvariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.SkeinMultisetInvariant","kind":"class","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrderingIndependence.lean#L88-L102","doc":"**Skein-multiset invariance — typeclass (Pattern 4).**\n\nPer-pair witness that the symmetric Skein polynomial agrees on\ntwo configurations sharing the same nucleon multiset. The\nuniversal claim depends on the `List.Perm.prod_eq`\n+ multiset-of-pairs argument not yet in Mathlib; until then,\ncallers supply the witness for the specific configurations they\nuse (typically a fixed nucleus where computational verification\nis available, e.g. tritium with 9 ordering checks). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.skeinPolynomialSym","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.skeinPolynomialSym","kind":"def","line":53,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/OrderingIndependence.lean#L53-L86","doc":"The Young-symmetrized Skein polynomial is defined as\n`P_sym(q) = tr_M(e_{(n)} · B(config) · e_{(n)})` where\n`e_{(n)}` is the symmetric Young idempotent and `B` is\nthe chain element for any ordering of `config`.\n\nThis is ordering-independent: the result depends only on\nthe multiset of nucleon types `{Z protons, N neutrons}`,\nnot on the specific ordering. ","signatureChunk":0}]}